Subject: Printing using HPDirects with Alpha
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96076332421626


Hi group

We are trying to get our remote printers set up using HP jetdirects with our 
new digital Alpha. The message we are receiving after we send out a print 
job is "waiting for Remote2 to come up"

We have set up the directories as required and gave it our ip addresses and 
even pinged the jetdirect. When we do an     lpc   status   we get the 
following

               printer is on remote host Remote2 with name hpjetex1
               queuing is enabled
               printing is enabled
               3 entries in the spool area
               Thurs May 30   : waiting for Remote2 to come up.

but still no print job sent to the HP540
We have used the following commands to print
          lpr   -PTRANSCRIPTS     .profile
          lp    -dTRANSCRIPTS     .profile

We have no problems with getting our serial printer to print, which is 
connected to the serial port or even getting a print job sent to our 1 
Digital Rapid Print Server. So we know that it will print.
Any suggestions on where else to look or what to do next? Any and all 
suggestions are appreciated.

Thank you.

Ron Mudge
mudger@odc.edu

From tru64-unix-managers  Thu May 30 17:11:28 1996
From: cnbv01 () puck ! eee ! strath ! ac ! uk
Date: Thu, 30 May 1996 17:11:28 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: Any recommended compilers
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96076332421647

Dear all,

I'm looking for suggestions on good C compilers for DEC Alpha boxes. We are 
currently running DU v3.2c

Some of the applications that our research group have been working on are being 
ported to PC's and other workstations. We have found ourselves in the 
embarassing situation that when compiled on these other platforms a whole host 
of warnings and even errors are thrown up by these other compilers. Clearly the 
standard DEC C compiler is very very tolerant of lazy/ambiguous/etc coding. Some 
of the stuff which has got through is unbelievable and rather frightening!

So can anyone tell me how far they have been able to crank up the warning level 
on the DEC C compiler, and if there are any more stringent compilers out there. 
Someone in our research group actually suggested using the DEC C++ compiler. Do 
you know if it is more pedantic than the C compiler?

Any guidance would be appreciated.

Regards,
Graeme
